<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vienna Secession</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Secession movement</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> Secession was an art movement founded in 1897 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-klimt">Gustav Klimt</a> and other artists who broke away from Vienna's established academic art association. It promoted modern styles in painting, architecture and design. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a> place it within the cultural flowering of late imperial Vienna.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vienna-secession.jpg" alt="Vienna Secession" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Ledl · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Secession_2016,_Vienna.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Vienna Secession, also called the Union of Austrian Artists, was founded on 3 April 1897 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-klimt">Gustav Klimt</a>, Koloman Moser, Josef Hoffmann, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-maria-olbrich">Joseph Maria Olbrich</a> and others, who resigned from the Association of Austrian Artists, the Künstlerhaus, in protest against its support for traditional styles. Otto Wagner joined shortly afterwards. The name came from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich-secession">Munich Secession</a> of 1892. Its stated aims included contact with artists outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, opposition to artistic nationalism, renewal of the decorative arts and a Gesamtkunstwerk, a total art uniting painting, architecture and the decorative arts. The group began with fifty members and elected Klimt its president, with Rudolf von Alt, aged eighty-five, as honorary president.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Olbrich's domed exhibition building of 1897 to 1898, with a frieze over the entrance, became the symbol of the movement and was the first dedicated gallery of contemporary art in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>. Between 1899 and 1903 it showed works by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claude-monet">Monet</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edgar-degas">Degas</a>, Renoir and other French artists. The 14th exhibition in 1902, designed by Hoffmann, was dedicated to Beethoven, with a statue by Max Klinger and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-klimt">Klimt</a>'s Beethoven Frieze. The group's magazine was Ver Sacrum.</p><p>Disputes arose after Hoffmann and Moser founded the Wiener Werkstätte in 1903. Members divided between those who put painting first and those who wanted equal treatment for the decorative arts. When a proposal to buy the Galerie Miethke was lost by one vote, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-klimt">Klimt</a>, Hoffmann, Moser and others resigned on 14 June 1905, and Klimt formed the Kunstschau in 1906.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Secession continued to hold exhibitions after 1905. Its building was destroyed under the Nazi regime as a symbol of degenerate art and rebuilt after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>; Hoffmann rejoined in 1945 and was president from 1948 to 1950. Wagner's later work, such as the Austrian Postal Savings Bank of 1903 to 1906, and Hoffmann's Stoclet Palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> moved away from floral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/art-nouveau">Art Nouveau</a> towards geometric forms and the beginnings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modernism">modernism</a>. Art Nouveau is named after the Vienna Secession in several languages of the former Austro-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, such as Polish secesja and Czech secese, and the movement influenced Młoda Polska.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vienna Secession” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Vienna's fin-de-siècle culture rested on this prosperity: the Ringstrasse, the <b>Secession movement</b>, coffee-house intellectual life and the professional class that produced Sigmund Freud, Gustav Mahler, Gustav Klimt and Theodor Herzl all belonged to a prosperous, cosmopolitan urban economy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
